BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20260905T041835Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53613865042535
DTSTART:20260827T220000Z
DTEND:20260827T233000Z
DESCRIPTION:Thurs. August 27\, 2026 | 6 pm - 7:30 pm | 109 E Jones St\, Ral
 eigh\, NC 27601\n\nEVENT OVERVIEW\n\nJoin the NC Department of Natural & C
 ultural Resources (DNCR) on August 27 from 6:00–7:30 p.m. for a FREE\, p
 owerful and inspiring evening celebrating the life and legacy of Mahalia J
 ackson\, widely considered the greatest gospel singer of her time and an o
 ften-overlooked leader in the American Civil Rights Movement.\n\nThis spec
 ial program features Mary D. Williams\, acclaimed gospel singer and schola
 r\, and Tim Tyson\, New York Times best-selling author\, co-authors of the
  new book Mahalia Jackson\, Moving On Up a Little Higher: The Story of an 
 American Civil Rights Pioneer. \n\nThrough presentation\, discussion\, and
  moments of live performance\, Williams and Tyson will discuss their groun
 dbreaking portrait of Jackson\, revealing not only her extraordinary music
 al gifts but also her essential influence within the Civil Rights Movement
 \, a legacy that still resonates powerfully today.\n\nFollowing their pres
 entation\, Dr. Darin Waters\, Deputy Secretary for DNCR’s Office of Arch
 ives and History\, will join the authors onstage for a conversation explor
 ing Jackson’s enduring impact on music\, social justice\, and American h
 istory\, with time reserved for audience questions.\n\nWe invite you to be
  part of this moving tribute to a woman whose voice and vision helped shap
 e a nation.\n\nBOOK SIGNING TO FOLLOW\n\nReleased on Aug. 18\, copies of M
 ahalia Jackson\, Moving On Up a Little Higher: The Story of an American Ci
 vil Rights Pioneer will be available for purchase at the event\, and the p
 rogram will end with a book signing.K-12 teachers who are currently employ
 ed in a NC public school can sign up at the start of the event to win a co
 py of the book! Name and a school e-mail address will be necessary to ente
 r. 10 copies will be awarded at the end of the evening. Teachers must be p
 resent to win.RSVP & SEATING\n\nPLEASE NOTE THAT SEATING IS FIRST COME\, F
 IRST SERVED. While filling out this form does not reserve seating\, you ar
 e helping us plan ahead so we can welcome all guests comfortably in our 20
 0‑seat auditorium for this complimentary event. Doors open at 5:30 p.m.\
 n\nLOCATION & PARKING\n\nThis event will take place in the DNCR Auditorium
  at 109 E Jones St\, Raleigh\, NC 27601. Parking is available in the Visit
 or's Lot directly across the street (free after 6:30 PM) or along the stre
 ets surrounding DNCR.ACCOMODATIONS\n\nIf you need assistance or accommodat
 ions to participate in this event\, please reach out at EducationOutreach@
 dncr.nc.gov so our staff can help.\n\nSPONSORS\n\nThis event is provided b
 y the NC Department of Natural & Cultural Resources (DNCR) Office of K-12 
 Education and the Come Hear NC Music Office\, a program of DNCR's NC Arts 
 Council.\n\nABOUT THE PRESENTERS\n\nMary D. Williams\nMary D. Williams is 
 a renowned gospel singer\, historian\, and educator from North Carolina wh
 o brings African American spirituals and Civil Rights–era anthems to lif
 e\, highlighting the connections between music and social change. Born in 
 Garner and rooted in community storytelling\, she performs widely across t
 he US\, while weaving history into her art. As a powerful voice in arts an
 d culture\, she builds community and fosters intergenerational understandi
 ng through performance and scholarship. She is the co-author of the new bo
 ok Mahalia Jackson\, Moving On Up a Little Higher: The Story of an America
 n Civil Rights Pioneer.\n\nTimothy B. Tyson \nTim Tyson is a senior resear
 ch scholar at Duke University’s Center for Documentary Studies and adjun
 ct professor of American Studies at UNC\, specializing in the intersection
 s of race\, religion\, and the Civil Rights Movement. A prize winning hist
 orian and author\, his books (including Blood Done Sign My Name and The Bl
 ood of Emmett Till) have earned national acclaim and major literary awards
 . He is the co-author of the new book Mahalia Jackson\, Moving On Up a Lit
 tle Higher: The Story of an American Civil Rights Pioneer.\n\nDr. Darin J.
  Waters\nDr. Darin Waters is the Deputy Secretary for the Office of Archiv
 es and History at DNCR\, overseeing the operations of the divisions of Sta
 te History and Maritime Museums\, State Historic Sites\, Archives and Reco
 rds\, and Historical Resources. He is also the secretary of the North Caro
 lina Historical Commission and the State Historic Preservation Officer. An
  accomplished historian and educator\, he formerly served as Associate Pro
 fessor at UNC Asheville and was Executive Director of UNCA’s Office of C
 ommunity Engagement. With a PhD from UNC Chapel Hill\, Waters has blended 
 academic expertise with public service throughout his career.
GEO:35.78292;-78.637155
LOCATION:NC Department of Natural and Cultural Resources\, Auditorium
SUMMARY:An Evening with Authors Tim Tyson & Mary D. Williams – Mahalia Ja
 ckson\, Moving On Up a Little Higher
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/an-evening-with-authors-tim-
 tyson-mary-d-williams-mahalia-jackson-moving-on-up-a-little-higher
CATEGORIES:Performances & Arts
CATEGORIES:Concerts (Music)
CATEGORIES:Education Programs
CATEGORIES:Panels & Book Discussions
END:VEVENT
